    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm working on a loft addition for a house in Oregon. i'm planning to use a Douglas Fir beam to span a 14ft gap. the loft is gonna hold a heavy bed and some storage, so i'm estimating a live load of about 40 psf and a dead load of 15 psf. can you help me figure out what size beam i should be looking at to stay within decent deflection limits? i don't want it to feel bouncy when someone walks up there.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To determine the size of the beam, you'll need to check the Oregon building codes for specific requirements. For a residential deck floor, you can reference the International Residential Code (IRC) Chapter 9. You'll need to calculate the total load of 40 psf + 15 psf = 55 psf, and then consider dead load and live load factors. A general rule of thumb is to use a beam with a 5 psf load factor to stay within deflection limits.",
